{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"The saved ground pour provides a continuous return through J1.3’s diagonal thermal spoke. The raw waveform gives 0.632120134 V at 1 ms, matching the independent RC calculation and meeting the declared range. Fresh ERC and DRC reports also contain zero violations; I haven’t found an evidence-supported defect."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/experiments/notes.md <<'MD'\n# Physical initial review experiment record\n\nCandidate: 1b342471005bae6c5d2029e39c033963daf85c8e69e3436446f63daf5b962f05\nRun: 1c24510a-0c7d-4b4b-82e5-6d7aba87f11c\nPacket: 8635a23254cbacf03e10a28c3c256b8780877d507ea324002095643ec7c3ad35\nRole: physical. Review date: 2026-10-16.\n\n## Commands and versions\n\n\"'`python3 /work/experiments/run_checks.py` records exact subprocess argv, environment overrides, exits and log filenames in commands.json. KiCad 10.0.7, ngspice 42; standard Python 3 used for physical_probe.py. The preliminary `/usr/bin/python3 -c` import probe reported Python 3.12.3, pcbnew 10.0.7; importing shapely failed (not installed). pcbnew emitted three PROPERTY_ENUM assertion diagnostics during import, also seen in supplied work/geometry/console.log. No defect is inferred from these diagnostics. The independent geometry calculation uses the Python standard library, not pcbnew or shapely.\n\nPreliminary read-only discovery: pwd; rg --files for AGENTS.md under /work, /candidate and /evidence (no matches); ls of /candidate/project, /evidence/work, /evidence/checks and /work/experiments. Read native requirements and project settings with cat. Read check records checks/drc.json, checks/geometry.json and checks/rc-step.json. `kicad-cli pcb drc --help` confirmed refill requires the explicit --refill-zones flag, which was never used. rg file discovery also enumerated supplied stock libraries; no credential files were opened. Native schematic and board were subsequently read in full by physical_probe.py. `nl -ba` inspected native PCB lines 564–788 and supplied step deck/log. No candidate writes or refill were performed.\n\n`python3 /work/experiments/physical_probe.py` produced physical_probe.json. The script is retained with exact equations, parser, raw decoder, sample spacing and polygon-distance method. All native track and pad coordinates are in mm. Its standard-library reader parses native s-expressions independently of the supplied pcbnew probe and compares pad net membership against a fresh native schematic XML export.\n\n## Results\n\n- Native PCB lines 667–705: all five straight track segments have width 0.25 mm, meeting the 0.20 mm requirement in inputs/requirements/fixture-requirements.md item 2. No copper arc tracks exist. One 0.6 mm diameter / 0.3 mm drill via is not counted as a track segment. This is geometry only, with no ampacity inference.\n- Fresh drc.json: violations=[], unconnected_items=[], schematic_parity=[]. Fresh erc.json: one sheet, violations=[]. These match work/drc/drc.json and work/erc/erc.json. Native project contains no drc_exclusions or additional declared rules. Ignored check lists are retained in reports; a zero exit is not used as electrical proof.\n- All seven pad net assignments match fresh schematic XML nets: /IN={J1.1,R1.1}, /OUT={J1.2,R1.2,C1.1}, GND={J1.3,C1.2}. Native segment centerline intersection graph independently joins all /IN and /OUT pads, including the /OUT branch joining the middle of a segment at (107.5,100).\n- Native PCB C1.2 connects on F.Cu to via b71a70d7-bbc1-43d1-ae55-5e4d992412a4 at (113,100); via spans F.Cu/B.Cu. Saved B.Cu zone 38a83012-bcf9-4bb9-b9f4-50d49cd91d90 (native lines 715–785) has a continuous polygon path from via annulus to J1.3 annulus. Retained polyline: (113.2,100)→(113.2,106.5)→(101.42,106.5)→(100.5,105.58). All samples at <=0.005 mm spacing lie inside saved fill, with >=0.207107 mm distance to polygon edges; the distance bound exceeds half sample spacing, ruling out an unsampled boundary crossing. Endpoints are outside drill holes and inside respective copper annuli. This proves existence of a saved copper return, not RF performance or physical plating continuity.\n- Initial attempted horizontal path ended in J1.3'\"'s thermal clearance and therefore failed containment. Its output is retained as physical_probe-initial-path.json. Follow-up radial samples at radius 0.7 mm every 15 degrees showed diagonal spokes (30–60, 120–150, 210–240, 300–330 degrees inside; cardinal directions outside). Native lines 749–765 corroborate these spokes. Selecting the southeast spoke gave the continuous path above. Failed choice of a test path is not an open circuit.\n- Placement: native board is a closed 20×12 mm rectangle (96,96)–(116,108). Courtyard extents: C1=(108.3,99.02)–(111.7,100.98), R1=(103.32,99.05)–(106.68,100.95), J1=(98.23,98.23)–(101.77,106.85). Courtyards do not intersect and lie within the board. Nominal header annular ring is 0.35 mm; nominal via annular ring is 0.15 mm. These are saved dimensions, not a manufacturer capability claim.\n- Native schematic R1=10k and C1=100n agree with fresh XML and work/rc-step/deck.cir lines 2–3. Thus tau=1 ms. Independently evaluated 1-exp(-t/tau) gives 0.632120558829 V at 1 ms and 0.993262053001 V at 5 ms. Deck line 4 uses a 1 ns PWL rise, initially zero volts, with DC operating point out=0 in work/rc-step/ngspice.log lines 17–24. Its finite-ramp analytic result is 0.632120374889 V at 1 ms (only 0.184 microvolt below ideal step).\n- Independently decoded work/rc-step/result.raw: 1025 real samples, time, v(in), v(out), i(vstep). Initial input/output=0. At 1 ms interpolated input=1 V, output=0.632120134017 V; at 5 ms output=0.993262119729 V. These agree with the supplied .meas values and analytic response. The 1 ms value is within 0.62–0.65 V. The 5 ms measurement has no declared pass/fail bound, so it is informational.\n- For the declared ideal step only: initial peak R current=100 microampere, initial R dissipation=0.1 mW, final stored capacitor energy=50 nJ. These calculations impose no component rating or thermal qualification claim.\n\nNo evidence-supported defect identified within the captured fixture scope.\n\n## Coverage omissions / assumptions\n\n- Only the ideal unloaded response is qualified analytically and against supplied simulation data; no hardware, tolerance, temperature, output loading, source impedance, ADC sampling, EMI, RF return quality or PCB parasitic extraction was tested.\n- Saved zone fill only; no refill or repaired candidate was generated.\n- No fabrication-output (Gerber/drill/paste) validation, fabrication process/capability audit, copper/plating thickness verification, drill/registration tolerance study, assembly yield, mechanical mating/enclosure or component-specific datasheet/BOM qualification. Saved footprints are generic; no part ratings are invented.\n- Track width is checked solely against the declared geometric minimum. No current-carrying, thermal or lifetime qualification follows from track width or the ideal power calculation.\n- Native checks retain ignored ERC categories: single_global_label, four_way_junction, simulation_model_issue, footprint_filter; DRC: missing_courtyard, track_not_centered_on_via, tuning_profile_track_geometries, footprint_filters_mismatch, footprint_type_mismatch. No claim of exhaustive checking of those categories. This board's native courtyards, via placement and pad types were inspected as geometry.\n- The independent native geometry script is intentionally limited to this board's unrotated footprints, straight signal tracks and single saved polygon. No general CAD parser/geometry validator claim. Polygon connectivity test certifies one existing return path, not every possible island or thermal process behavior; full saved-native DRC is complementary.\n- No fresh ngspice run: the existing binary waveform and .meas were independently checked against native values and closed-form step/ramp responses. Ngspice version was independently obtained. The raw decoder assumes this real little-endian double-precision ngspice format; header count, variable names, initial values and analytic results corroborate it.\n- No external references were needed or fetched; no prior-review experiments or judgments were consulted.\nMD\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport hashlib,json\np=Path('/work/experiments')\nh=json.loads((p/'commands.json').read_text())['input_hashes']\nprint('Candidate input hashes unchanged:',all(hashlib.sha256((Path('/candidate')/f).read_bytes()).hexdigest()==v for f,v in h.items()))\nprint('Experiment files:', len(list(p.glob('*'))))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
